<section id="sec-mistakes" class="col">
  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul class="tight">
    <li><strong>The line load taken for the whole load.</strong> The value of \(q\) is put into a balance as if it were a force. You catch it by looking at the unit, because a line load is in N/m and only becomes a force in N when it has been multiplied by a length. A close relative is multiplying by the length of the beam where the load covers only a part of it, which you catch by reading off the drawing how long the load itself is.</li>
    <li><strong>The resultant in the wrong place.</strong> It is put at the middle of the beam although the load covers only a part of it, or the distance from \(A\) to the start of the load is forgotten. You catch it by marking the resultant on the drawing before any moment is taken, and by imagining a short stack right next to one support, which must send nearly everything to that support.</li>
    <li><strong>One third and two thirds swapped.</strong> The resultant of a rising load is placed one third of the way from its low end. You catch it by remembering that the resultant must lie on the side where most of the load is, and that is the high end.</li>
    <li><strong>The resultant used for more than the reactions.</strong> The spread load is replaced by its resultant, and the same picture is then used to judge how hard the beam is bent. You catch it by keeping the two questions apart, since the resultant answers what the supports carry and nothing else.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

